{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-112615" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the polynomial $P(x)$ given that\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}-13{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+17x-6=(x-3)(x+1)P(x).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-112615" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $f(x)=3{x}^{4}-13{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+17x-6.$ We know that $(x-3)$ and $(x+1)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-3)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $3$ | $-13$ | $7$ | $17$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $9$ | $-12$ | $-15$ | $6$ |\n|  | $3$ | $-4$ | $-5$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{4}-13{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+17x-6}{x-3}=3{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-5x+2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}-13{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+17x-6=(x-3)(3{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-5x+2).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x+1)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(3{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-5x+2).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $3$ | $-4$ | $-5$ | $2$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-3$ | $7$ | $-2$ |\n|  | $3$ | $-7$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-5x+2}{x+1}=3{x}^{2}-7x+2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}-13{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+17x-6=(x-3)(x+1)(3{x}^{2}-7x+2).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}-13{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+17x-6=(x-3)(x+1){{(3x-1)(x-2)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=(3x-1)(x-2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-112615" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "813fc008d5363ba96665e4e4e7b141dfa41ac3217322ab0c0e843b2dc9ac000b"} {"question" "q-112615" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-112615" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(2x+1)(x+2)"] ["math" "(2x-1)(x+2)"] ["math" "(3x+1)(x-2)"] ["math" "(3x-1)(x-2)"] ["math" "(4x-1)(x-1)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-112615" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(3x-1)(x-2)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-18862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the polynomial $P(x)$ given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}+9x+18=(x-2)(x-3)P(x).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-18862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $f(x)={x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}+9x+18.$ We know that $(x-2)$ and $(x-3)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-3)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-11$ | $9$ | $18$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $3$ | $6$ | $-15$ | $-18$ |\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $-5$ | $-6$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}+9x+18}{x-3}={x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-5x-6,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}+9x+18=(x-3)({x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-5x-6).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x-2)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $({x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-5x-6).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x-2)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-5$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $2$ | $8$ | $6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $4$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-5x-6}{x-2}={x}^{2}+4x+3,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}+9x+18=(x-2)(x-3)({x}^{2}+4x+3).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}+9x+18=(x-2)(x-3){{(x+1)(x+3)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=(x+1)(x+3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-18862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "813fc008d5363ba96665e4e4e7b141dfa41ac321732