 | $3$ | $4$ | $-37$ | $-14$ | $24$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $9$ | $39$ | $6$ | $-24$ |\n|  | $3$ | $13$ | $2$ | $-8$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-37{x}^{2}-14x+24}{x-3}=3{x}^{3}+13{x}^{2}+2x-8,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-37{x}^{2}-14x+24=(x-3)(3{x}^{3}+13{x}^{2}+2x-8).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x+4)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(3{x}^{3}+13{x}^{2}+2x-8).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x+4)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-4$ | $3$ | $13$ | $2$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-12$ | $-4$ | $8$ |\n|  | $3$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{3}+13{x}^{2}+2x-8}{x+4}=3{x}^{2}+x-2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-37{x}^{2}-14x+24=(x-3)(x+4)(3{x}^{2}+x-2).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-37{x}^{2}-14x+24=(x-3)(x+4){{(3x-2)(x+1)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=(3x-2)(x+1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-112617" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "813fc008d5363ba96665e4e4e7b141dfa41ac3217322ab0c0e843b2dc9ac000b"} {"question" "q-112617" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-112617" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(2x+3)(x-1)"] ["math" "(2x-3)(x-1)"] ["math" "(3x+2)(x+1)"] ["math" "(3x-2)(x+1)"] ["math" "(4x-3)(x+2)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-112617" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(3x-2)(x+1)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-328505" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The polynomial \n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+16x-12\n$$\n\n \ncan be expressed as\n\n$$\n(x-2)(x-3)\\cdot P(x).\n$$\n\nFind the polynomial $P(x).$\n\n$P(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-328505" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $f(x)={x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+16x-12.$ We know that $(x-2)$ and $(x-3)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-2)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $16$ | $-12$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $2$ | $-4$ | $-10$ | $12$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-2$ | $-5$ | $6$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+16x-12}{x-2}={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-5x+6,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+16x-12=(x-2)({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-5x+6).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x-3)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-5x+6).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-5$ | $6$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $3$ | $3$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-5x+6}{x-3}={x}^{2}+x-2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+16x-12=(x-2)(x-3)({x}^{2}+x-2).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(x)={x}^{2}+x-2=(x+2)(x-1).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-328505" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "813fc008d5363ba96665e4e4e7b141dfa41ac3217322ab0c0e843b2dc9ac000b"} {"question" "q-328505" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-328505" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x^2+x-2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-112626" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the polynomial $P(x)$ given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-36{x}^{2}-2x+35=(x-5)(x+1)P(x).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-112626" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $f(x)={x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-36{x}^{2}-2x+35.$ We know that $(x-5)$ and $(x+1)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-5)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-5)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-5)$ using synthetic division:\n\n\\\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $5$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-36$ | $-2$ | $35$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $5$ | $35$ | $-5$ | $-35$ |\n|  | $1$ | $7$ | $-1$ | $-7$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-36{x}^{2}-2x+35}{x-5}={x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-x-7,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-36{x}^{2}-2x+35=(x-5)({x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-x-7).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x+1)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $({x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-x-7).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $7$ | $-1$ | $-7$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-1$ | $-6$ | $7$ |\n|  | $1$ | $6$ | $-7$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-x-7}{x+1}={x}^{2}+6x-7,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-36{x}^{2}-2x+35=(x-5)(x+1)({x}^{2}+6x-7).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-36{x}^{2}-2x+35=(x-5)(x+1){{(x-1)(x+7)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=(x-1)(x+7).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-112626" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "813fc008d5363ba96665e4e4e7b141dfa41ac3217322ab0c0e843b2dc9ac000b"} {"question" "q-112626" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-112626" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(2-x)(x+1)"] ["math" "(x-1)(5-x)"] ["math" "(x-1)(x+7)"] ["math" "(x-5)(x+7)"] ["math" "(x-7)(x+1)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-112626" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(x-1)(x+7)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-112638" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $p(x)={x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-35{x}^{2}-57x+90.$ Given that $p(-3)=p(1)=0,$ find the sum of all distinct real roots of $p(x).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-112638" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $p(-3)=p(1)=0,$ by factor theorem $(x+3)$ and $(x-1)$ are both factors of $p(x).$\nTo factor $p(x)$ completely we need to find its other factors.\n\nSince $(x-1)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x-1)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-35$ | $-57$ | $90$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-33$ | $-90$ |\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $-33$ | $-90$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-35{x}^{2}-57x+90}{x-1}={x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-33x-90,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-35{x}^{2}-57x+90=(x-1)({x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-33x-90).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x+3)$ is also a factor of $p(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $({x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-33x-90).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x+3)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-3$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-33$ | $-90$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-3$ | $3$ | $90$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-1$ | $-30$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-33x-90}{x+3}={x}^{2}-x-30,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-35{x}^{2}-57x+90=(x-1)(x+3)({x}^{2}-x-30).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-35{x}^{2}-57x+90=(x-1)(x+3)(x-6)(x+5).\n$$\n\nWe can now equate each factor to zero and solve for $x$ to find the three roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =1\\,,\\,x=-3\\,,\\,x=6\\,,\\,x=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the roots of the polynomial is\n\n$$\n1-3+6-5=-1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-112638" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "813fc008d5363ba96665e4e4e7b141dfa41ac3217322ab0c0e843b2dc9ac000b"} {"question" "q-112638" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-112638" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-112638" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-270967" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $p(x)={x}^{4}-5{x}^{2}+4,$ and $p(1)=p(-1)=0.$ In **ascending order**, the distinct real roots of $p(x)$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}, \n\n$\\,x=$\n\n{{field-2}}, \n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-3}}, \n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-4}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-270967" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $p(1)=p(-1)=0,$ by factor theorem $(x-1)$ and $(x+1)$ are both factors of $p(x).$\nTo factor $p(x)$ completely we need to find its other factors.\n\nSince $(x-1)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x-1)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-5$ | $0$ | $4$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-4$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ | $-4$ | $-4$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{4}-5{x}^{2}+4}{x-1}={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-4x-4,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-5{x}^{2}+4=(x-1)({x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-4x-4).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x+1)$ is also a factor of $p(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $({x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-4x-4).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-4$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $4$ |\n|  | $1$ | $0$ | $-4$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-4x-4}{x+1}={x}^{2}-4,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-5{x}^{2}+4=(x-1)(x+1)({x}^{2}-4).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-5{x}^{2}+4=(x-1)(x+1)(x-2)(x+2).\n$$\n\nWe can now equate each factor to zero and solve for $x$ to find the four roots. In ascending order, the roots are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-2\\,,\\,x=-1\\,,\\,x=1\\,,\\,x=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-270967" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "813fc008d5363ba96665e4e4e7b141dfa41ac3217322ab0c0e843b2dc9ac000b"} {"question" "q-270967" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-270967" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-270967" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-270967" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-270967" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-270967" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-270967" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-270967" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-337081" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $p(x)=9{x}^{4}+42{x}^{3}+37{x}^{2}-28x-12,$ and $p(-2)=p(-3)=0.$ In **ascending order**, the distinct real roots of $p(x)$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}, \n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}, \n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-3}}, \n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-4}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-337081" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $p(-2)=p(-3)=0,$ by factor theorem $(x+2)$ and $(x+3)$ are both factors of $p(x).$\nTo factor $p(x)$ completely we need to find its other factors.\n\nSince $(x+2)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x+2)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $9$ | $42$ | $37$ | $-28$ | $-12$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-18$ | $-48$ | $22$ | $12$ |\n|  | $9$ | $24$ | $-11$ | $-6$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{9{x}^{4}+42{x}^{3}+37{x}^{2}-28x-12}{x+2}=9{x}^{3}+24{x}^{2}-11x-6,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n9{x}^{4}+42{x}^{3}+37{x}^{2}-28x-12=(x+2)(9{x}^{3}+24{x}^{2}-11x-6).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x+3)$ is also a factor of $p(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(9{x}^{3}+24{x}^{2}-11x-6).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x+3)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-3$ | $9$ | $24$ | $-11$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-27$ | $9$ | $6$ |\n|  | $9$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{9{x}^{3}+24{x}^{2}-11x-6}{x+3}=9{x}^{2}-3x-2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n9{x}^{4}+42{x}^{3}+37{x}^{2}-28x-12=(x+2)(x+3)(9{x}^{2}-3x-2).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n9{x}^{4}+42{x}^{3}+37{x}^{2}-28x-12=(x+2)(x+3)(3x+1)(3x-2).\n$$\n\nWe can now equate each factor to zero and solve for $x$ to find the four roots. In ascending order, the roots are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-3\\,,\\,x=-2\\,,\\,x=-\\frac{1}{3}\\,,\\,x=\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-337081" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "813fc008d5363ba96665e4e4e7b141dfa41ac3217322ab0c0e843b2dc9ac000b"} {"question" "q-337081" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-337081" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-337081" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-337081" "f