stion" "q-112651" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $f(x)={x}^{4}-10{x}^{3}+34{x}^{2}-90x+225.$ We know that $(x-5)^{2}$ is a factor of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-5)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-5)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-5)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $5$ | $1$ | $-10$ | $34$ | $-90$ | $225$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $5$ | $-25$ | $45$ | $-225$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-5$ | $9$ | $-45$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{4}-10{x}^{3}+34{x}^{2}-90x+225}{x-5}={x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+9x-45,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-10{x}^{3}+34{x}^{2}-90x+225=(x-5)({x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+9x-45).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x-5)^{2}$ is a factor of $f(x),$ $(x-5)$ must be also a factor of the quotient $({x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+9x-45).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x-5)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $5$ | $1$ | $-5$ | $9$ | $-45$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $5$ | $0$ | $45$ |\n|  | $1$ | $0$ | $9$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+9x-45}{x-2}={x}^{2}+9,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-10{x}^{3}+34{x}^{2}-90x+225=(x-5)^{2}({x}^{2}+9).\n$$\n\nTo see whether the quadratic polynomial ${x}^{2}+9$ can be factored any further, we calculate its discriminant:\n\n$$\n{b}^{2}-4ac={0}^{2}-4(1)(9)=-36<0\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic polynomial ${x}^{2}+9$ cannot be factored over the real numbers since its discriminant is negative. \n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ over the real numbers is given by \n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-10{x}^{3}+34{x}^{2}-90x+225=(x-5)^{2}{{({x}^{2}+9)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $P(x)={x}^{2}+9.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-112651" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "813fc008d5363ba96665e4e4e7b141dfa41ac3217322ab0c0e843b2dc9ac000b"} {"question" "q-112651" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-112651" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{x}^{2}+2"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+3"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+9"] ["math" "{x}^{2}-3x+9"] ["math" "{x}^{2}-4x+6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-112651" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{x}^{2}+9"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-270972" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the polynomial $P(x)$ given that $2{x}^{4}-7{x}^{3}-34{x}^{2}+56x-192=(x+4)(x-6)P(x).$\n\n$P(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-270972" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{4}-7{x}^{3}-34{x}^{2}+56x-192.$ We know that $(x+4)$ and $(x-6)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x+4)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x+4)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-4$ | $2$ | $-7$ | $-34$ | $56$ | $-192$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-8$ | $60$ | $-104$ | $192$ |\n|  | $2$ | $-15$ | $26$ | $-48$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{x}^{4}-7{x}^{3}-34{x}^{2}+56x-192}{x+4}=2{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+26x-48,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n2{x}^{4}-7{x}^{3}-34{x}^{2}+56x-192=(x+4)(2{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+26x-48).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x-6)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(2{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+26x-48).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x-6)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $6$ | $2$ | $-15$ | $26$ | $-48$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $12$ | $-18$ | $48$ |\n|  | $2$ | $-3$ | $8$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+26x-48}{x-6}=2{x}^{2}-3x+8,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{4}-7{x}^{3}-34{x}^{2}+56x-192 & =(x+4)(x-6)(2{x}^{2}-3x+8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo see whether the quadratic polynomial $2{x}^{2}-3x+8$ can be factored any further, we calculate its discriminant:\n\n$$\n{b}^{2}-4ac=(-3)^{2}-4(2)(8)=-55<0\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic polynomial $2{x}^{2}-3x+8$ cannot be factored over the real numbers since its discriminant is negative. \n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ over the real numbers is given by \n\n$$\n2{x}^{4}-7{x}^{3}-34{x}^{2}+56x-192=(x+4)(x-6){{(2{x}^{2}-3x+8)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $P(x)=2{x}^{2}-3x+8.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-270972" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "813fc008d5363ba96665e4e4e7b141dfa41ac3217322ab0c0e843b2dc9ac000b"} {"question" "q-270972" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "q-270972" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2x^2-3x+8"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/state.json" "file_sha256" "d625c6e80ad85e4f0d8343f5c24c5580f5f4bb5c9445fb124fe36bf09c0140ff"} {"question" "e-6258" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $(x-1)$ and $(x-3)$ are factors of the polynomial $f(x)= x^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6,$ factor $f(x)$ completely." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/example-6258.json" "file_sha256" "e63f2fd51ce31e9518fda861ed7b1f0c4ee2607ade9cfe60948184eb4568b211"} {"question" "e-6258" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To factor $f(x)$ completely, we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-1)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^4$ | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-7$ | $1$ | $6$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-7$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $0$ | $-7$ | $-6$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6}{x-1} = x^3-7x-6,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6 = (x-1) (x^3-7x-6).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x-3)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(x^3-7x-6).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}3$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-7$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $3$ | $9$ | $6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $3$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^3-7x-6}{x-3} = x^2+3x +2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6 =  (x-1) (x-3) (x^2+3x +2).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6 =  (x-1) (x-3) (x+2)(x+1).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/example-6258.json" "file_sha256" "e63f2fd51ce31e9518fda861ed7b1f0c4ee2607ade9cfe60948184eb4568b211"} {"question" "e-6259" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $p(x) = x^4 - x^3 - 9 x^2 - 11 x - 4.$ Knowing that $p(4) = p(-1) = 0,$ factor the polynomial $p(x)$ completely." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/example-6259.json" "file_sha256" "d5e05e50ad1f3930df0fd30b0dec00e6135eda2a4bab63a8d62475cc48ab5fcc"} {"question" "e-6259" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $p(4) = p(-1) = 0,$ the factor theorem guarantees that $(x - 4)$ and $(x + 1)$ are both factors of $p(x).$\nTo factor $p(x)$ completely, we need to find its other factors.\n\nSince $(x - 4)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x - 4)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x - 4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^4$ | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}4$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-9$ | $-11$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $4$ | $12$ | $12$ | $4$ |\n|  | $1$ | $3$ | $3$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^4 - x^3 - 9 x^2 - 11 x - 4}{x-4} = x^3+3x^2+3x+1,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 9 x^2 - 11 x - 4 = (x-4) (  x^3+3x^2+3x+1).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x+1)$ is also a factor of $p(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $( x^3+3x^2+3x+1).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $3$ | $1$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $-1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{ x^3+3x^2+3x-1}{x+1} = x^2+2x+1,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 9 x^2 - 11 x - 4 = (x-4)(x+1)(x^2+2x+1).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}-11x-4 & =(x-4)(x+1)(x+1)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-4)(x+1)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/example-6259.json" "file_sha256" "d5e05e50ad1f3930df0fd30b0dec00e6135eda2a4bab63a8d62475cc48ab5fcc"} {"question" "e-6203" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x) = 3 x^4 - 5 x^3 - 3 x^2 + 6 x - 8.$ If $x=2$ and $x=-\\dfrac{4}{3}$ are zeros of $f(x),$ factor $f(x)$ completely." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/example-6203.json" "file_sha256" "b045bc940e79a3bafd8f4b5971cc1298cfd07de1e6f80f99b56f3d0b3491a517"} {"question" "e-6203" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Because $x=2$ and $x=-\\dfrac{4}{3}$ are both zeros of $f(x),$ the factor theorem guarantees that $(x-2)$ and $\\left( x + \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)$ are both factors of $f(x).$ So, $(x-2)$ and $\\left( x + \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)$ both divide $f(x).$\n\nTo begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by one of these factors (it doesn't matter which one we divide first). Let's divide $f(x)$ by $(x-2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^4$ | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}2$ | $3$ | $-5$ | $-3$ | $6$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $6$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $8$ |\n|  | $3$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $4$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3{x}^{4}-5{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+6x-8 \\\\  & =(x-2)(3{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, because $\\left( x+\\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $3x^3 + x^2 - x +4.$ So, we divide this quotient by $\\left( x+\\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}-\\dfrac{4}{3}$ | $3$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $4$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $-4$ | $4$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $3$ | $-3$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-x+4 & =(x+\\frac{4}{3})(3{x}^{2}-3x+3) \\\\  & =(x+\\frac{4}{3})(3)({x}^{2}-x+1) \\\\  & =(3x+4)({x}^{2}-x+1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{4}-5{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+6x-8 & =(x-2)(3x+4)({x}^{2}-x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe quadratic polynomial $x^2-x+1$ does not appear to factor over the real numbers. We can make sure of this by verifying that its discriminant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={b}^{2}-4ac=(-1)^{2}-4(1)(1)=-3<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ over the real numbers is given by \n\n$$\nf(x) = (x-2)(3x+4)(x^2-x+1).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066299/example-6203.json" "file_sha256" "b045bc940e79a3bafd8f4b5971cc1298cfd07de1e6f80f99b56f3d0b3491a517"}] "usage" {"e-6258" [] "e-6259" [] "e-6203" []} "basis" 2185 "retractions" [[17592186054487 :question/problem "Given that $(x-1)$ and $(x-3)$ are factors of the polynomial $f(x)= x^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6$, factor $f(x)$ completely."] [17592186054487 :question/worked-solution "To factor $f(x)$ completely, we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x)$, we know that $(x-1)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x)$, we can divide it by $(x-1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^4$ | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{red}1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-7$ | $1$ | $6$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-7$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $0$ | $-7$ | $-6$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6}{x-1} = x^3-7x-6\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6 = (x-1) (x^3-7x-6)\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x-3)$ is also a factor of $f(x)$, it must be a factor of the quotient $(x^3-7x-6)$. So, we divide this quotient by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{red}3$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-7$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $3$ | $9$ | $6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $3$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^3-7x-6}{x-3} = x^2+3x +2\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6 = (x-1) (x-3) (x^2+3x +2)\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6 = (x-1) (x-3) (x+2)(x+1)\n$$"] [17592186054488 :question/problem "Let $p(x) = x^4 - x^3 - 9 x^2 - 11 x - 4$. Knowing that $p(4) = p(-1) = 0$, factor the polynomial $p(x)$ completely."] [17592186054488 :question/worked-solution "Since\n\n$$\np(4) = p(-1) = 0\n$$\n\nthe factor theorem guarantees that $(x - 4)$ and $(x + 1)$ are both factors of $p(x)$.\nTo factor $p(x)$ completely, we need to find its other factors.\n\nSince $(x - 4)$ is a factor of $p(x)$, we know that $(x - 4)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x)$, we can divide it by $(x - 4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^4$ | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{red}4$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-9$ | $-11$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $4$ | $12$ | $12$ | $4$ |\n|  | $1$ | $3$ | $3$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^4 - x^3 - 9 x^2 - 11 x - 4}{x-4} = x^3+3x^2+3x+1\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 9 x^2 - 11 x - 4 = (x-4) (x^3+3x^2+3x+1)\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x+1)$ is also a factor of $p(x)$, it must be a factor of the quotient $(x^3+3x^2+3x+1)$. So, we divide this quotient by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{red}-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $3$ | $1$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $-1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^3+3x^2+3x-1}{x+1} = x^2+2x+1\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 9 x^2 - 11 x - 4 = (x-4)(x+1)(x^2+2x+1)\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{4} - x^{3} - 9x^{2} - 11x - 4 &= (x - 4)(x + 1)(x + 1)^{2} \\\\\n&= (x - 4)(x + 1)^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186054486 :question/problem "Let $f(x) = 3 x^4 - 5 x^3 - 3 x^2 + 6 x - 8$. If $x=2$ and $x=-\\dfrac{4}{3}$ are zeros of $f(x)$, factor $f(x)$ completely."] [17592186054486 :question/worked-solution "Because $x=2$ and\n\n$$\nx=-\\dfrac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nare both zeros of $f(x)$, the factor theorem guarantees that $(x-2)$ and\n\n$$\n\\left(x + \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)\n$$\n\nare both factors of $f(x)$. So, $(x-2)$ and\n\n$$\n\\left(x + \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)\n$$\n\nboth divide $f(x)$.\n\nTo begin factoring $f(x)$, we can divide it by one of these factors (it doesn't matter which one we divide first). Let's divide $f(x)$ by $(x-2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^4$ | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{red}2$ | $3$ | $-5$ | $-3$ | $6$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $6$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $8$ |\n|  | $3$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $4$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= 3x^{4} - 5x^{3} - 3x^{2} + 6x - 8 \\\\\n&= (x - 2)(3x^{3} + x^{2} - x + 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, because\n\n$$\n\\left(x+\\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)\n$$\n\nis also a factor of $f(x)$, it must be a factor of the quotient $3x^3 + x^2 - x +4$. So, we divide this quotient by\n\n$$\n\\left(x+\\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)\n$$\n\nusing synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{red}-\\dfrac{4}{3}$ | $3$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $4$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $-4$ | $4$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $3$ | $-3$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x^{3} + x^{2} - x + 4 &= (x + \\frac{4}{3})(3x^{2} - 3x + 3) \\\\\n&= (x + \\frac{4}{3})(3)(x^{2} - x + 1) \\\\\n&= (3x + 4)(x^{2} - x + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x^{4} - 5x^{3} - 3x^{2} + 6x - 8 &= (x - 2)(3x + 4)(x^{2} - x + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe quadratic polynomial $x^2-x+1$ does not appear to factor over the real numbers. We can make sure of this by verifying that its discriminant is negative:\n\n$$\nD = b^{2} - 4ac = (-1)^{2} - 4(1)(1) =-3 < 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ over the real numbers is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) = (x-2)(3x+4)(x^2-x+1)\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
